.\" Written by hand from lib/common.sh (require_config, build_exclude_params)
.\" and libexec/archivectl/*.sh; keep it in step with them.
.TH ARCHIVECTL.BORGBACKUP 5 2026-11-14 "archivectl" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
archivectl.borgbackup, .borgbackup \- backup source configuration for archivectl
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I <directory>/.borgbackup
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archivectl initialise
writes
.I .borgbackup
into the directory it is to back up.
Every other command of
.BR archivectl (1)
finds the nearest one in the current directory or its parents, changes
into the directory that holds it, and reads it with the shell's
.B source
builtin: the file is a bash script, run with the user's rights, and may
set any variable.
.PP
These variables are read:
.TP
.B BORG_REPO
The Borg repository.
Required by every command but
.BR initialise ,
which sets it to
.IR $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name> .
.TP
.B SOURCE_DIR
What
.B create
archives,
.B diff
compares and
.B restore
extracts into, relative to the directory of the file.
Required by those three;
.B initialise
sets it to
.BR ./ .
.TP
.B EXCLUDES
A bash array of patterns
.B create
passes to
.B borg create
as
.BI \-\-exclude " pattern"
each, and
.B diff
to
.B rsync
as
.BI \-\-exclude= pattern
when it compares the live source.
When it is not set the patterns are
.BR .cache ,
.B tmp
and
.BR __pycache__ .
.PP
A file that is missing, or that leaves a required variable empty, ends
the command with status 3.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# Borg backup configuration for /home/user/papers
SOURCE_DIR="./"
BORG_REPO="/home/user/.borg_backups/!home!user!papers"
EXCLUDES=(".cache" "node_modules" "*.log")
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR archivectl (1),
.BR borg (1)
